  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 404 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nThrough tonight: Heat and humidity will be the main concern in the \nnear term. Early morning satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed the \ncirculation around a large 598dam high centered over the central \nPlains. Our CWA will remain on the eastern periphery of this large \nhigh through the forecast period. A couple disturbances were noted \ndropping around the eastern side of the high. One such disturbance \nwas over northeast Mississippi early this morning and local radars \nshowed scattered light showers over our northeast zones. This \nactivity will continue into this morning before ending as the \ndisturbance shifts east of Mississippi. Early morning surface \nanalysis had high centered over the northern Gulf. This surface high \nwill remain in place through the forecast period. Together high \npressure surface and aloft will contribute to temperatures topping \nout a couple degrees warmer than on Saturday. This heat will combine \nwith high humidity to lead to heat index values above 105F over most \nof the CWA. Our northwest will see heat index values greater than \n110F. There won't be much relief from the humidity tonight. A record \nwarm low temperature was tied at Greenville Saturday with several \nclimate sites coming within a couple degrees of setting or tying a \nrecord for warm morning lows. This trend will continue tonight as \nwell. /22/\n\nThrough at least mid-week, high levels of both heat and humidity \nwill yield a lengthy period of dangerous heat stress across the \nforecast area. High temperatures will top out in the upper 90s on \nMonday, and upper 90s to just above 100F Tuesday into Wednesday. \nThese temperatures, in addition to dew points in the 70s, will \nresult in afternoon heat index values at and above 106F across the \nCWA.\n\nAs a result, the \"Heat Advisory\" for Sunday has been extended \nthrough 9 PM Monday with indices between 106-110F for at least a \ncouple of hours. The exception to this is across Bolivar, Sunflower, \n& Leflore counties where afternoon heat index values will climb \nabove 110 for an hour or two. Here, an \"Extreme Heat Warning\" has \nbeen extended until 9 PM Monday.\n\nAs this dangerous heat stress increases Monday through mid-week, a \ncontinuation of the \"Heat Advisory\" into the work week is expected, \nwith further expansion of the \"Extreme Heat Warning\" across the CWA \nlikely.  This will be messaged in the latest HWO graphic. However at \nthe moment, an assessment of the layout and continuation of heat-\nrelated products will be made on a daily basis.  Therefore, please \ncontinue to monitor the latest forecast for the most up-to-date \ninformation. \n\nAt the end of the forecast period, next Thursday into Friday, the \naforementioned high aloft will weaken its presence across eastern \nportion of the region.  This will allow troughing along the east \ncoast to deepen across the Southeast United States. As this happens, \nanother surface boundary will attempt to sink south into the CWA. \nThus, increasing rain chances across the area and possibly bringing \nat least some temporary relief to the main weather focus of this \nforecast period...the oppressive heat./19/22/",
